        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objective:</B><BR>
From a basic biological research perspective, identification of gravity-dependent 

physiological processes may add significantly to our overall understanding of how the 

environment, and gravity in particular, affects the basic processes of life.  Bacteria and 

yeast are some of the simplest forms of life, yet they exhibit physiological pathways that 

have been conserved through millions of years of evolution.  In this way, microbes represent 

the ideal model for studying the most basic life processes.  The simplicity of these 

organisms allows for targeting of specific bioprocesses without the complications of 

intercellular and system-level interactions.  Gravity represents an under-utilized tool in 

the array of environmental parameters used to elicit metabolic responses.  Gravity is unique 

in this array in that it is the one environmental constant under which all forms of life 

evolved.  For this reason, researchers believed it would be especially intriguing to observe 

how microbes adapt to an environmental parameter, which their genetic programming had never 

encountered. 
<P>
Microorganisms are capable of quickly adapting to changes in their environment.  Metabolic 

pathways adjust to new environmental conditions to optimize growth and ensure survival.  

Environmental adaptation is a key tool used in microbial research to investigate the basic 

physiology of microbial species.  Temperature, pH, and growth media manipulation are classic 

examples of environmental parameter control techniques used to induce observable metabolic 

changes.  While such classic induced physiology adaptation has been studied extensively, the 

effects of gravity on microbial physiology remain essentially unknown.  Prior spaceflight 

research indicates that exposure to microgravity can lead to increased growth rates and 

increased resistance to antimicrobial agents.  However, the limited data and conflicting 

results of this body of work demand that a well-controlled, prolific methodology be adapted 

for microbial spaceflight research.  This experiment screened for statistically significant 

differences in physiological behavior between ground-based and on-orbit microbial cultures.  

Researchers investigated the growth, substrate utilization characteristics, and reaction to 

antimicrobial agents of various bacteria and yeasts.  The hypothesis tested was that 

microgravity would induce fundamental changes in microbial physiology.  
<P>
<B>Approach:</B><BR>
Due to hardware commonality, this experiment was teamed with the experiment &#34;Space 

Flight Effects on Fungal Growth, Metabolism, and Sensitivity to Antifungal Agents 

(9601240)&#34; on STS-107, and the experiments were jointly referred to as Microbial 

Physiology Flight Experiments (MPFE).  For this experiment, the following bacteria and 

yeasts were evaluated using the Automated Microbiology System (AMS): 
